**Fix** — `src/backoff.js`, `rateLimitDelay`: it returned `retryAfterMs` alone, so a rate-limited (429) error effectively waited 0 ms and the worker hammered the upstream. It now takes the longest of the exponential schedule, the fixed 1 s rate-limit floor, and any upstream `Retry-After`:

```js
export function rateLimitDelay(config, attempt, retryAfterMs = 0) {
  const floorMs = 1000
  return Math.max(delayFor(config, attempt), floorMs, retryAfterMs)
}
```

Fatal client errors (400/422/401/… ) were already classified and skipped without retry by `classify`, so the rejected-payload vs. slow-down distinction was otherwise intact.

All 23 tests pass. Single-line fix in `src/backoff.js`:

```js
export function rateLimitDelay(config, attempt, retryAfterMs = 0) {
  return Math.max(delayFor(config, attempt), 1000, retryAfterMs)
}
```

`rateLimitDelay` previously returned only `retryAfterMs` (0 when absent), so 429s retried with no wait. It now takes the longer of the exponential schedule, a 1s floor, and any `Retry-After` value. The 400/422/401 fatal path in `classify` was already correct, so rejected payloads stay fatal and are never retried.

