That only uppercases the character *after* a separator. For a snake_case key (`line_total`) it works because the word bodies are already lowercase and only the boundary letter needs changing. But for a `SCREAMING_SNAKE` storage column (`LINE_TOTAL`) there's no lowercase anywhere, so it produced `LINETOTAL` instead of `lineTotal`. Because all four modules share that one converter, a single missed style let storage-style names leak from every payload — matching the reported symptom.

## Fix

`src/wire.js` — normalize an all-caps key to lowercase before camelizing, leaving camelCase input (and its humps) untouched:

```js
export function toCamel(key) {
  const str = String(key)
  // A SCREAMING_SNAKE key has no case boundaries left once the separators are
  // consumed, so lower it first and camelise the words. camelCase input keeps
  // its humps because only the all-caps form is normalised.
  const normalized = /^[A-Z0-9_-]+$/.test(str) ? str.toLowerCase() : str
  return normalized.replace(/[-_]+([a-zA-Z0-9])/g, (whole, char) => char.toUpperCase())
}
```

This makes the documented three input styles (`snake_case`, `SCREAMING_SNAKE`, `kebab-case`) all converge on camelCase: `line_total`/`LINE_TOTAL`/`line-total` → `lineTotal`, while `id`, `sku`, `camelCase` and the nested address/lines are preserved. The wire round-trip (`toWire` → `fromWire`) still restores the original snake_case rows.

